# Agent guidance for {{name}}

This file is the single source of truth for AI coding agents in this repo. `CLAUDE.md`,
`GEMINI.md`, `.cursorrules` and `.github/copilot-instructions.md` are symlinks to it, and
`.claude/skills` and `.gemini/skills` are symlinks to `.agents/skills`. `ge doctor` holds
them.

**This file is the ROUTER, not the manual.** It says what the project is, the rules every task
follows, where things live, and which skill owns each subject. The procedures live in
`.agents/skills/<name>/SKILL.md`. Load the skill that owns your task's subject before your first
edit.

## What this is

{{name}} is a game written in C++20 on the engine at {{engine_repo}}, pinned to one release in
`cmake/engine.cmake`. The engine gives the deterministic core (`ge::core`), the lab framework
(`ge::lab`), its plugins (`ge::<plugin>`) and the `ge` command line this repo's checks run on.

## Rules for every task

- **DETERMINISTIC.** The simulation (`sim/`) produces the same bits for the same seed and
  inputs on every platform: time is the step count, every random draw comes from the state's
  `ge::core::Rng`, transcendentals come from `core/libm/libm.hpp`, and no build flag loosens the
  FP flags `ge::core` carries. `ge doctor` holds it (skills: `engine`, `sim-system`).
- **LABS, NOT GUESSES.** A change in a lab's subject runs the lab before and after and puts both
  in the PR (skill: `labs`).
- **ERRORS ARE PROMPTS.** Every error and warning says what failed, where, and the next action
  (skill: `write-code`, § Diagnostics). `ge doctor` holds every message to it.
- **THE ENGINE IS NOT PATCHED HERE.** A bug in the engine is reported upstream with a repro
  (skill: `engine`); the build never edits `build/*/_deps/`.
- **THIS REPOSITORY MAY BE PUBLIC.** No tokens, keys, e-mail addresses or home paths in the tree,
  in workflows or in their logs; credentials live in the CI's secrets.
- **SKILLS LEARN.** Read each loaded skill's lessons before the first edit, and record what the
  session learned before the commit (skill: `skill-reflection`).
- **Every work session ends with the `commit` skill.**

## Build, test and grow it: ge

`ge` is this repo's one command line (skill: `ge`): every command takes `--json` and answers
with findings that say what failed, where and the next action. The pinned release builds its own
`ge` into `build/<preset>/ge`; before the first build, use a release package's `ge` (or
`make ge-build`).

```sh
ge check                         # the gate: format, ge doctor, build, every test and lab smoke
ge build | ge test [--filter=x]  # (--preset=release: -O2, no sanitizers)
ge lab [<id>] [--tag=before | --compare=before] [-- <the lab's flags>]
ge find <words> | ge explain <file|name>   # this repo's and the pinned engine's code
ge add system|lab|test <name>    # a working piece to start from, tags filled
ge use <plugin>                  # link one of the engine's plugins, install its skill
ge doctor | ge skills sync | ge lessons <skill>
ge help [<command>] [--json]     # the whole catalog
```

`make` targets remain for people (`make test`, `make fmt` formats every file at once); an agent
uses `ge`.

## Where things live

| Path | What | Skill |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `sim/` | The simulation: the state, the fixed step, every system the game has | `sim-system` |
| `labs/` | The labs: one `<id>_lab.cpp` each, all in the `labs` executable | `labs` |
| `tests/` | `<topic>_test.cpp`, one executable each; `check.hpp` is the harness | `sim-system` |
| `cmake/engine.cmake` | The engine pin: the release, its checksum | `engine` |
| `.agents/skills/` | The skills: the engine's (synced) and this repo's own | `skill-reflection` |

Every source file ends its leading comment with a `ge:` tag block (`ge:summary`, `ge:keywords`,
`ge:skill`, `ge:test`), so `ge find <word>` finds it (skill: `write-code`).

## Skills

Skills live in `.agents/skills/`, each a `SKILL.md` with `name` and `description` front matter
and a `.lessons/` directory. Lessons are this repo's; the engine's skills below are rewritten
by `ge skills sync` to the pinned release, so never edit one in place: write a lesson.

